Method chaining in Java is the practice of calling different methods in a single line instead of calling other methods with the same object reference separately. Each method in chaining returns an object, allowing multiple method calls to be invoked one after another on the same object without intermediate variables. The syntax is: obj.method1().method2().method3(); where an object (obj) calls method1(), then method2(), then method3() sequentially. Method chaining is also known as parameter idiom or named parameter idiom, and sometimes as a train wreck due to long chains of method calls. For example, in the provided code:

class A {
    private int a;
    private float b;
    A() { System.out.println("Calling The Constructor"); }
    int setint(int a) { this.a = a; return this.a; }
    float setfloat(float b) { this.b = b; return this.b; }
    void display() { System.out.println(...); }
}

However, note that the example methods return primitive types (int, float), not the object itself, which typically breaks true method chaining that requires returning the current object (this).

To implement Comparable in Java, a class must implement the Comparable<T> interface and override the compareTo() method to define natural ordering. For example, the Movie class implements Comparable<Movie> and provides a compareTo() method that sorts movies by their release year:

class Movie implements Comparable<Movie> {
    private String name;
    private double rating;
    private int year;
    public Movie(String name, double rating, int year) {
        this.name = name;
        this.rating = rating;
        this.year = year;
    }
    // Implementation of the compareTo method for default sorting by year
    public int compareTo(Movie m) {
        return this.year - m.year;
    }
}

This allows sorting using Collections.sort() or Arrays.sort(), as the class now defines its own natural order. The compareTo() method returns a negative integer, zero, or a positive integer if the current object is less than, equal to, or greater than the specified object. Comparable is used for a single, default sorting order within the class itself.

An enum in Java is a special data type that cannot be used to create objects and cannot extend other classes (but it can implement interfaces). An enum can, just like a class, have attributes and methods. The only difference is that enum constants are public, static and final (unchangeable - cannot be overridden). Use enums when you have values that you know aren't going to change, like month days, days, colors, deck of cards, etc. The values() method returns an array of all enum constants and is useful when you want to loop through the constants of an enum:  
for (Level myVar : Level.values()) {  
  System.out.println(myVar);  
}  
The output will be:  
LOW  
MEDIUM  
HIGH

